= Turing completeness — Simple =

A system is '''Turing complete''' if, with enough idealized time and memory, it can perform any computation that a general Turing machine can perform.

This does not mean the system is fast.

It does not mean it is easy to program.

It does not mean every question about its programs can be solved.

Some tiny or deliberately strange programming languages are Turing complete.

Some useful systems are intentionally less powerful because restrictions can make them easier to analyze.

